🚽 Test case: issues #272/#273 -- INT_MIN / -1 and INT_MIN % -1 crashed
🚽 with SIGFPE.
🚽
🚽 A zero-divisor check alone does not cover integer division: on x86-64
🚽 `idiv` raises #DE for INT_MIN / -1 too, because the mathematical
🚽 quotient (2147483648) is not representable in int. C leaves it
🚽 undefined; the hardware traps.
🚽
🚽 The modulo form traps for the same reason even though its own answer is
🚽 fine -- `idiv` produces quotient and remainder together, so the
🚽 unrepresentable QUOTIENT faults even when only the remainder was asked
🚽 for.
🚽
🚽 ── The two cases are deliberately handled differently ────────────────
🚽 Division reports an error and falls back to 0, matching how division by
🚽 zero has always behaved here: there is no correct int to return, so
🚽 saying so is the honest answer.
🚽
🚽 Modulo returns 0 with NO error, because 0 is the mathematically correct
🚽 remainder and it is exactly representable. Nothing is lost, so there is
🚽 nothing to diagnose -- the trap was purely an artifact of how the
🚽 hardware computes it. A fixture that expected an error here would be
🚽 pinning a worse implementation.
🚽
🚽 INT_MIN is built by subtraction rather than written as a literal
🚽 because every integer literal is lexed with atoi() into an int
🚽 (lang.l), so -2147483648 cannot be spelled directly -- see #282.
🚽
🚽 smol is NOT affected and is included to pin that: C's usual arithmetic
🚽 conversions promote both shorts to int before dividing, so -32768 / -1
🚽 is computed where the result IS representable and never traps.
🚽
🚽 ── What each printed line actually pins ──────────────────────────────
🚽 The two are not equally strong, and it is worth being exact about
🚽 which is which (PR #310 review).
🚽
🚽 `mod: 0` does real work, because what this fixture pins about the
🚽 modulo case is the ABSENCE of a diagnostic. Make INT_MIN % -1 report
🚽 an error -- the tempting "consistency" change, since its sibling does
🚽 -- and stderr gains a line the expected output does not have, so the
🚽 fixture fails. That is the regression most likely to be attempted
🚽 here, and it is caught.
🚽
🚽 `div: 0` pins the diagnostic and the absence of a crash, but NOT that
🚽 the fallback was written: `result` comes from SAFE_MALLOC and
🚽 safe_free() memsets blocks before releasing them, so an
🚽 uninitialized read yields 0 too. Deleting `*(int *)result = 0;` from
🚽 the guard leaves this fixture green -- verified by mutation. The
🚽 shipped code does set it; the fixture simply cannot see the
🚽 difference, and should not be read as claiming otherwise.
skibidi main {
    rizz lo = 0 - 2147483647;
    lo = lo - 1;
    rizz neg = 0 - 1;

    yapping("div: %d", lo / neg);
    yapping("mod: %d", lo % neg);

    smol slo = 0 - 32768;
    smol sneg = 0 - 1;
    yapping("smol mod: %d", slo % sneg);
    bussin 0;
}
